WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.979
The software stack begins with an operating-system image.

00:00:02.979 --> 00:00:06.217
Provider images may include GPU drivers and acceleration libraries,

00:00:06.217 --> 00:00:08.017
while general images require installation.

00:00:08.017 --> 00:00:11.975
Match the NVIDIA driver, CUDA runtime, framework, and GPU architecture deliberately.

00:00:11.975 --> 00:00:15.933
A command that detects the GPU is only the first test;

00:00:15.933 --> 00:00:20.611
confirm that the chosen framework can allocate memory and execute a representative operation.

00:00:20.611 --> 00:00:23.490
Containers package applications and dependencies into repeatable units,

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:28.168
but they still rely on a compatible host driver and GPU container runtime.

00:00:28.168 --> 00:00:32.846
Pin image versions or immutable digests instead of pulling an uncontrolled latest tag.

00:00:32.846 --> 00:00:36.804
For non-container workflows, create isolated Python environments and record package versions.

00:00:36.804 --> 00:00:38.603
Notebooks are useful for exploration,

00:00:38.603 --> 00:00:44.361
but a long-lived notebook with manual changes is difficult to reproduce and easy to expose accidentally.

00:00:44.361 --> 00:00:47.239
Model servers turn the instance into a service,

00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:50.478
and local API endpoints let applications communicate with them.

00:00:50.478 --> 00:00:55.876
Bind development services to a private interface or loopback address until access controls are ready.

00:00:55.876 --> 00:00:59.834
Document model versions, startup arguments, ports, context settings, and health checks.

00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:03.792
If a web notebook or model interface must be reached remotely,

00:01:03.792 --> 00:01:08.830
prefer an authenticated private access path over opening a management port to the internet.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:12.069
Automate setup with reviewed scripts, images, or infrastructure definitions.

00:01:12.069 --> 00:01:17.466
The goal is to replace a failed or temporary instance without reconstructing it from memory.

00:01:17.466 --> 00:01:20.345
Keep secrets and private data outside reusable images.

00:01:20.345 --> 00:01:23.943
Test the build on a fresh instance, measure provisioning time,

00:01:23.943 --> 00:01:27.182
and preserve only the components necessary for the workload.

00:01:27.182 --> 00:01:31.860
Reproducibility reduces both operational risk and the billable time spent repairing one-off environments.
